WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.393 --> 00:00:01.875
- I'm not the smartest kid in the world.

00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:02.708
Okay.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:05.497
Instead of somebody sayin',
"Oh no, you're smart.

00:00:05.497 --> 00:00:06.975
"No, no, don't say that to yourself."

00:00:06.975 --> 00:00:09.603
I said to myself, "No,
I'm a dumb (silence)."

00:00:09.603 --> 00:00:10.548
Okay, roger that.

00:00:10.548 --> 00:00:12.235
And I think I'm the
hardest guy in the world.

00:00:12.235 --> 00:00:13.181
You got to believe that.

00:00:13.181 --> 00:00:14.493
You got to believe you are something.

00:00:14.493 --> 00:00:16.050
The whole thing about comfort zones is

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.958
if you live in one for too
long, that becomes your norm.

00:00:19.958 --> 00:00:20.820
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:21.653
It's Evan.

00:00:21.653 --> 00:00:24.355
My One Word is Believe,
and I believe that you have

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:26.573
the ability to do something
special that can change

00:00:26.573 --> 00:00:27.517
the planet.

00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:29.158
And so, to help you on your
journey, today, we're going to

00:00:29.158 --> 00:00:31.705
learn from Navy Seal and
Ultra Marathon runner,

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:35.862
David Goggins, and my take on
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:37.342
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:37.342 --> 00:00:40.298
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:40.298 --> 00:00:42.961
(Swoosh)

00:00:42.961 --> 00:00:46.878
(majestic instrumental melody)

00:00:53.365 --> 00:00:57.307
- I came home one night from
work, sprayin' for cockroaches,

00:00:57.307 --> 00:01:01.658
and long story short, I turned
on the Discovery Channel

00:01:01.658 --> 00:01:05.320
and I saw some guys going
through Navy Seal training.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.535
And they were going through
Hell Week and they were

00:01:06.535 --> 00:01:08.427
gettin' their ass just beat.

00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:12.822
You know, in and out of the
water, guys ringin' the bell.

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:17.760
There was sufferin', and I
was weighin' like 297 pounds.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.396
And I had to make a change in my life.

00:01:20.396 --> 00:01:21.229
You know?

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:24.922
I was at an all time low,
and I wasn't goin' anywhere,

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:27.137
and I was exactly what everybody
said I was going to be,

00:01:27.137 --> 00:01:28.502
which was nothin'.

00:01:28.502 --> 00:01:30.029
So, I had to make a change.

00:01:30.029 --> 00:01:31.427
- What was it about seeing suffering?

00:01:31.427 --> 00:01:33.363
This is really interesting,
and I actually get it

00:01:33.363 --> 00:01:34.801
but I want to hear you explain it.

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:39.654
Why suffering was was the thing
that triggered that thought?

00:01:39.654 --> 00:01:41.009
- Well, for me, growin' up,

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:42.820
I came from a horrible background.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.366
I got called Nigger every
day of my life growin' up.

00:01:45.366 --> 00:01:46.977
Lived in a small town.

00:01:46.977 --> 00:01:51.452
The Klan headquarters
at that time was about

00:01:51.452 --> 00:01:54.174
20 minutes from where I lived.

00:01:54.174 --> 00:01:58.397
One of the high ups in the
KKK's son sat behind me

00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:00.987
in two classes, so he called
me Nigger all the time.

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:03.088
Got my first car, they
spray painted, "Nigger,

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:04.440
"we're going to kill you," on it.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.607
So, I was just a insecure, scared kid.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.670
And the only way I could find myself

00:02:12.657 --> 00:02:14.418
was through puttin' myself thorough

00:02:14.418 --> 00:02:16.022
the worst thing possible.

00:02:16.022 --> 00:02:18.242
I want to say the exact
opposite of what the world,

00:02:18.242 --> 00:02:19.801
today's world, is saying.

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:21.734
So we read a bunch of books nowadays.

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:25.901
As humans, we want to find
out how to be someone else.

00:02:27.296 --> 00:02:30.589
What we don't do is we don't go inside,

00:02:30.589 --> 00:02:33.839
so literally turn yourself
inside out, read the book that's,

00:02:33.839 --> 00:02:35.769
like we're writin' a book
every day of our lives.

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:38.610
Everyday, we're seein'
who we are as people.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.723
When I was growin' up, I
lied for people to accept me,

00:02:42.723 --> 00:02:44.456
because I didn't accept myself.

00:02:44.456 --> 00:02:46.434
So, I would make up
stories so then you would

00:02:46.434 --> 00:02:49.385
accept me into your world.

00:02:49.385 --> 00:02:52.374
Everything I did was for
someone else to like me.

00:02:52.374 --> 00:02:54.017
It wasn't until I started
reading my own book

00:02:54.017 --> 00:02:56.235
about how pathetic I was as a human being.

00:02:56.235 --> 00:02:58.703
I could blame my dad, I
could blame kids at school,

00:02:58.703 --> 00:03:01.500
I could blame havin' health
issues, ADD, my mom not

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.612
bein' around.

00:03:02.612 --> 00:03:04.502
Great mom but she was doin' her thing.

00:03:04.502 --> 00:03:05.335
- Right.

00:03:05.335 --> 00:03:06.729
- I could blame a lot of people,

00:03:06.729 --> 00:03:09.523
and that's the book I was reading.

00:03:09.523 --> 00:03:11.291
And I put it off on everybody else.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:13.957
It wasn't 'til I said, "You
know what, for me to fix this,

00:03:13.957 --> 00:03:15.971
"I got to read what the hell,
what the (silence) is wrong

00:03:15.971 --> 00:03:16.956
"with David Goggins."

00:03:16.956 --> 00:03:21.206
Not blame anybody, read my
book and say, "Okay, I'm afraid

00:03:21.206 --> 00:03:23.012
"of my shadow.

00:03:23.012 --> 00:03:25.236
"How can I overcome that?"

00:03:25.236 --> 00:03:27.708
Go in the military, get
your ass kicked, do things

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:29.150
you hate to do.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.536
Be uncomfortable every
(silence) day of your life.

00:03:31.536 --> 00:03:32.772
Roger that.

00:03:32.772 --> 00:03:34.909
I'm not the smartest kid in the world.

00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:36.234
Okay.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:37.798
Instead of somebody sayin,
"Oh no, you're smart.

00:03:37.798 --> 00:03:39.443
"No, no, don't say that to yourself."

00:03:39.443 --> 00:03:41.876
I said to myself, "No,
I'm a dumb (silence)."

00:03:41.876 --> 00:03:42.947
Okay, roger that.

00:03:42.947 --> 00:03:44.553
How you get smarter?

00:03:44.553 --> 00:03:46.687
Educate yourself.

00:03:46.687 --> 00:03:49.115
So the things that we
run from, we're runnin'

00:03:49.115 --> 00:03:51.085
from the truth.

00:03:51.085 --> 00:03:52.941
We're runnin' from the truth, man.

00:03:52.941 --> 00:03:56.520
So, the only way I became
successful was goin' towards

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.588
the truth.

00:03:57.588 --> 00:04:01.671
As painful and as brutal
as it is, it changed me.

00:04:02.731 --> 00:04:06.114
It allowed me to become, in my own right,

00:04:06.114 --> 00:04:07.628
who I am today.

00:04:07.628 --> 00:04:10.668
- David, tell 'em about the cookie jar.

00:04:10.668 --> 00:04:13.545
- The cookie jar is somethin' I invented.

00:04:13.545 --> 00:04:16.216
As you all know, your
mom has a cookie jar,

00:04:16.216 --> 00:04:18.521
and sometimes you might have
a Oreo, sometimes you might

00:04:18.521 --> 00:04:21.399
have a Chips Ahoy, sometimes
you might have a oatmeal

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:22.232
raisin cookie.

00:04:22.232 --> 00:04:23.065
You never know.

00:04:23.065 --> 00:04:24.524
It's just in there.

00:04:24.524 --> 00:04:25.607
My cookie jar

00:04:26.529 --> 00:04:31.121
has every single failure
and success of my life.

00:04:31.121 --> 00:04:32.927
Somethin' I overcame.

00:04:32.927 --> 00:04:36.421
So, what happens in time of
life when you're stressed out

00:04:36.421 --> 00:04:39.835
and things get bad, even the
hardest guy in the world,

00:04:39.835 --> 00:04:42.260
me, everybody thinks, and
I think I'm the hardest guy

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.034
in the world, you've got to believe that.

00:04:44.034 --> 00:04:45.926
You got to believe you are somethin'.

00:04:45.926 --> 00:04:49.593
I will, in my mind,
reach into my cookie jar

00:04:51.064 --> 00:04:54.645
and sometimes you forget how
hard you are in times of need.

00:04:54.645 --> 00:04:57.156
'Cause you're stressed.

00:04:57.156 --> 00:04:57.989
But you forget.

00:04:57.989 --> 00:04:59.992
I'm a (silence) Navy Seal, I'm
a Ranger, I'm this and that,

00:04:59.992 --> 00:05:03.035
but you forget all that
'cause your life sucks.

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:07.202
I calm down, take that one
second, get control of my life,

00:05:08.542 --> 00:05:10.542
reach in the cookie jar,

00:05:11.756 --> 00:05:13.403
"Wow, you got called
Nigger your whole life

00:05:13.403 --> 00:05:15.663
"and you're now the only
person in history to do"

00:05:15.663 --> 00:05:17.348
this, this, and this.

00:05:17.348 --> 00:05:20.146
Put it back in the cookie
jar, reset my mind.

00:05:20.146 --> 00:05:23.647
You have to remind yourself
of how badass you really are

00:05:23.647 --> 00:05:24.827
in times of need.

00:05:24.827 --> 00:05:25.660
(applause)

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:26.929
That's the (silence) cookie jar.

00:05:26.929 --> 00:05:29.846
- So many of us are on cruise
control, myself included,

00:05:29.846 --> 00:05:31.818
and we get into our daily routine.

00:05:31.818 --> 00:05:33.920
And routines are unbelievable,
but routines can also

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:34.870
be a rut.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.456
Only if you really challenge
yourself and push yourself

00:05:37.456 --> 00:05:40.591
and get out of your
routine, your comfort zone,

00:05:40.591 --> 00:05:41.865
can you get better.

00:05:41.865 --> 00:05:44.418
Like, you can't improve
when you're on auto pilot.

00:05:44.418 --> 00:05:48.404
So you have to set goals, and
you have to set challenges

00:05:48.404 --> 00:05:50.415
that you don't necessarily
think you can do.

00:05:50.415 --> 00:05:53.574
Or you're going to push yourself
way further than you think

00:05:53.574 --> 00:05:54.973
you can to improve.

00:05:54.973 --> 00:05:57.557
So, you got to get out of the
complacency of the routine.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:06:00.033
- The whole thing about comfort
zones is if you live in one

00:06:00.033 --> 00:06:02.709
for too long, that becomes your norm.

00:06:02.709 --> 00:06:05.885
It never allows you to get
experience, gain knowledge,

00:06:05.885 --> 00:06:08.726
gain any kind of mental toughness.

00:06:08.726 --> 00:06:10.494
- Well, I learned that we
all have a reserve tank

00:06:10.494 --> 00:06:12.095
that we're all capable
of doing so much more

00:06:12.095 --> 00:06:13.534
than we think we can.

00:06:13.534 --> 00:06:16.413
And only by really tapping
into that reserve tank,

00:06:16.413 --> 00:06:19.004
whether it's in business,
whether it's in physically,

00:06:19.004 --> 00:06:22.457
you want to get more fit, only
when you really push yourself

00:06:22.457 --> 00:06:25.129
and push your limits,
can you find out what

00:06:25.129 --> 00:06:27.142
you're really made of and
what's in your reserve tank.

00:06:27.142 --> 00:06:29.446
And I mean, he taught me
that every single day,

00:06:29.446 --> 00:06:32.690
and it just led to me getting
better in the various buckets

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.353
in my life as I tapped
into that reserve tank.

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:37.250
- Yeah, I think I said to
him, "Don't be a (bleep)."

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.062
- You know, we call it the 40% rule.

00:06:39.062 --> 00:06:41.733
That's somethin' that
David kind of told me

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:43.784
and shared with me, and I bought into it.

00:06:43.784 --> 00:06:45.389
But it's proved effective.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:47.778
Not just in working out,
but even in business,

00:06:47.778 --> 00:06:48.808
or my family life.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:51.112
It's like, when I'm
frustrated, when I'm done,

00:06:51.112 --> 00:06:54.897
when I hit a road block,
when things get complicated,

00:06:54.897 --> 00:06:57.038
you have so much more in you.

00:06:57.038 --> 00:06:58.843
- People have said, "Man, you
cuss all the (silence) time.

00:06:58.843 --> 00:07:00.281
"Why?"
(laughs)

00:07:00.281 --> 00:07:03.253
Well, I hate to say it,
the best way for me to get

00:07:03.253 --> 00:07:06.010
how I feel acrossed, I
can't sit here and say,

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.053
"You know what, yeah, I
went through Hell Week

00:07:07.053 --> 00:07:08.574
"and man, it was really hard."

00:07:08.574 --> 00:07:09.767
(chuckles)

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:11.173
No, that (silence)

00:07:11.173 --> 00:07:15.407
takes your damn soul, rips it inside out,

00:07:15.407 --> 00:07:19.977
and then they say, "Now, we're
going to (silence) start."

00:07:19.977 --> 00:07:23.227
It allows me to express where I was at,

00:07:24.457 --> 00:07:26.622
at a point of my life.

00:07:26.622 --> 00:07:30.652
If I don't give you all of
me, why the hell am I here?

00:07:30.652 --> 00:07:33.287
How will you learn from me?

00:07:33.287 --> 00:07:35.629
People take so much offense to me.

00:07:35.629 --> 00:07:38.349
You will never learn from
people if we always tap dance

00:07:38.349 --> 00:07:39.462
around the truth.

00:07:39.462 --> 00:07:41.271
- Oh God, I love that, so true.

00:07:41.271 --> 00:07:44.132
- We tap dance around the truth
by findin' the right words

00:07:44.132 --> 00:07:46.802
so I don't hurt you,
'cause you have thin skin.

00:07:46.802 --> 00:07:48.635
No, tighten up people.

00:07:49.798 --> 00:07:50.829
It's okay.

00:07:50.829 --> 00:07:52.392
Trust me, it's okay.

00:07:52.392 --> 00:07:54.155
You might be called Nigger one day.

00:07:54.155 --> 00:07:54.988
It's okay.

00:07:56.376 --> 00:07:58.966
You might be called some
Jewish word or some faggot

00:07:58.966 --> 00:07:59.799
or gay word.

00:07:59.799 --> 00:08:00.898
It's okay.

00:08:00.898 --> 00:08:01.880
Let 'em call you that.

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:03.485
What are you going to do now?

00:08:03.485 --> 00:08:04.928
They don't own your life.

00:08:04.928 --> 00:08:06.822
How are you going to control that now?

00:08:06.822 --> 00:08:08.594
How are you going to flip
it upside down and say,

00:08:08.594 --> 00:08:09.869
"Roger that.

00:08:09.869 --> 00:08:12.455
"Now I'm going to harness
this (silence) and you'll read

00:08:12.455 --> 00:08:14.631
"about me years from now."

00:08:14.631 --> 00:08:15.863
How?

00:08:15.863 --> 00:08:16.696
That's the question.

00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:17.835
How are you going to do that?

00:08:17.835 --> 00:08:19.725
Thicken your skin.

00:08:19.725 --> 00:08:21.254
Become more of a human being.

00:08:21.254 --> 00:08:24.329
Don't be afraid of the
reflection in the mirror,

00:08:24.329 --> 00:08:26.629
'cause that's all you can be afraid of.

00:08:26.629 --> 00:08:29.937
Once you overcome the
reflection in the mirror,

00:08:29.937 --> 00:08:31.477
you've done it.

00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:35.838
I literally broke all the
metatarsals in my feet.

00:08:35.838 --> 00:08:38.471
Stress fractures, shin
splints, everything, the pain

00:08:38.471 --> 00:08:41.275
goin' through my body, I've
been through every training

00:08:41.275 --> 00:08:42.108
known to man.

00:08:42.108 --> 00:08:43.826
Ranger school, Hell Week three
times, all kind of stuff.

00:08:43.826 --> 00:08:46.464
This event I'm explaining
to you right now,

00:08:46.464 --> 00:08:48.640
not enough time to explain
it to you in detail,

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.995
but it was the worst thing
I've done in my entire life

00:08:49.995 --> 00:08:51.354
pain wise.

00:08:51.354 --> 00:08:53.248
This is when I realized
a human being is capable

00:08:53.248 --> 00:08:56.414
of going to limits unknown to most people.

00:08:56.414 --> 00:08:58.270
This is where hardness comes in.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.121
People can read a book
on hardness and tell you

00:09:00.121 --> 00:09:02.555
about mental toughness and
hardness, but until you put

00:09:02.555 --> 00:09:05.308
your body through hardness,
and realize that there's walls

00:09:05.308 --> 00:09:07.781
and doors you must break
through, and once you break

00:09:07.781 --> 00:09:12.267
through them you are amazing,
and your body resets.

00:09:12.267 --> 00:09:14.808
But we are scared to get to that wall

00:09:14.808 --> 00:09:16.333
'cause that wall says what?

00:09:16.333 --> 00:09:17.166
"Stop."

00:09:17.166 --> 00:09:18.351
Right?

00:09:18.351 --> 00:09:20.364
But every wall has a what?

00:09:20.364 --> 00:09:21.197
A door.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.887
You got to get that key from
your pocket and don't be scared

00:09:24.887 --> 00:09:26.248
to see what's on the
other side of that door.

00:09:26.248 --> 00:09:30.415
- I would like to know when
you're in that moment failing,

00:09:31.798 --> 00:09:35.965
and you have to keep going,
what do you say to yourself?

00:09:38.081 --> 00:09:39.081
- "What if?"

00:09:41.541 --> 00:09:44.131
A lot of times I'll be in
tours at my run or somethin'

00:09:44.131 --> 00:09:46.881
like that, and I'm all jacked up.

00:09:48.037 --> 00:09:51.359
Body's broken, mind's
broken, spirit's broken.

00:09:51.359 --> 00:09:53.660
I started to say, "What
if I can pull this off?"

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.495
When I first walked in to the
Navy Seal recruiter's office,

00:09:56.495 --> 00:09:58.184
he looked at me and
said, "There's only be 35

00:09:58.184 --> 00:10:01.262
"African Americans in 70
years make it through."

00:10:01.262 --> 00:10:03.108
You know what I said to myself?

00:10:03.108 --> 00:10:05.873
"What if I can be the 36th?"

00:10:05.873 --> 00:10:09.775
It's the "What if I can pull
off a (silence) miracle?"

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:12.689
What if I can become
someone that no one thinks

00:10:12.689 --> 00:10:13.721
I can be?

00:10:13.721 --> 00:10:16.392
And just that, just me talkin' about that,

00:10:16.392 --> 00:10:18.251
I have the hair goin' up on my arms.

00:10:18.251 --> 00:10:22.568
'Cause it makes me just like,
"What if I can be that guy

00:10:22.568 --> 00:10:24.871
"that people who called
Nigger," and this and that,

00:10:24.871 --> 00:10:29.316
and now, I'm speakin' at
Tom Ferry's (silence) event.

00:10:29.316 --> 00:10:32.441
(applause and cheers)

00:10:32.441 --> 00:10:34.498
What if?
- Thank you.

00:10:34.498 --> 00:10:35.476
- That's it.

00:10:35.476 --> 00:10:38.810
So the first Ultra Marathon
wasn't smart at all.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:39.643
At all.

00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:43.624
So basically what happened
was, I was at military

00:10:43.624 --> 00:10:46.914
free fall school with Morgan Luttrell.

00:10:46.914 --> 00:10:48.725
Marcus Luttrell, if you guys don't know,

00:10:48.725 --> 00:10:52.550
was the lone survivor, the
guy, he was in a bad op,

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:55.597
op went bad, he was the
only Navy Seal that lived.

00:10:55.597 --> 00:10:57.076
Long story short.

00:10:57.076 --> 00:10:57.977
Got to get the book.

00:10:57.977 --> 00:10:59.905
Read Lone Survivor, great story.

00:10:59.905 --> 00:11:02.333
Morgan is Marcus Luttrell's twin brother.

00:11:02.333 --> 00:11:03.730
And I was there with Marcus.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:05.965
So, what happened was, myself and Morgan

00:11:05.965 --> 00:11:09.754
were in free fall school at
the same exact time Marcus

00:11:09.754 --> 00:11:12.762
was in the worst incident in Seal history.

00:11:12.762 --> 00:11:15.229
So, I knew that Marcus might be dead.

00:11:15.229 --> 00:11:17.163
He wasn't dead, everybody else was dead.

00:11:17.163 --> 00:11:20.934
So, I actually brought,
I actually told Morgan,

00:11:20.934 --> 00:11:23.194
"Hey man, your brother
was in a bad incident.

00:11:23.194 --> 00:11:25.538
"I don't know if he's alive,
I don't know what's goin' on."

00:11:25.538 --> 00:11:29.564
Long story short, Marcus
is alive and I go on

00:11:29.564 --> 00:11:31.623
to want to raise money for families.

00:11:31.623 --> 00:11:33.679
All these guys died, they all had kids.

00:11:33.679 --> 00:11:34.512
I want to raise money for

00:11:34.512 --> 00:11:36.920
the Special Operations Warrior Foundation.

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.045
It's a foundation where 100% tuition goes

00:11:40.045 --> 00:11:43.711
to these kids to go to college,
you know, full tuition,

00:11:43.711 --> 00:11:45.069
whatever.

00:11:45.069 --> 00:11:46.851
So I found this great foundation.

00:11:46.851 --> 00:11:48.388
I'm going to raise money for it.

00:11:48.388 --> 00:11:49.676
So, I said, "You know what?

00:11:49.676 --> 00:11:51.828
"I have to Google something that's evil.

00:11:51.828 --> 00:11:52.986
"Somethin' very hard."

00:11:52.986 --> 00:11:54.769
I knew nothin' about Ultra Marathons.

00:11:54.769 --> 00:11:56.296
I hadn't even run a marathon.

00:11:56.296 --> 00:11:58.526
I knew nothing about this world.

00:11:58.526 --> 00:12:02.628
So, I Googled the top 10
hardest races in the world.

00:12:02.628 --> 00:12:05.272
And what comes up is a Bad Water 135.

00:12:05.272 --> 00:12:08.984
So, 135 mile race through Death
Valley in the summer time.

00:12:08.984 --> 00:12:11.253
I thought it was a stage race.

00:12:11.253 --> 00:12:13.771
I thought it was a race
where you run like, 20 miles,

00:12:13.771 --> 00:12:14.771
set up camp,

00:12:15.632 --> 00:12:19.767
barbecue outside, and then go
run some more the next day.

00:12:19.767 --> 00:12:21.796
So, I called the race director
up at the race and said,

00:12:21.796 --> 00:12:23.655
"Hey Chris," his name is Chris Costen.

00:12:23.655 --> 00:12:25.099
"I want to do your race."

00:12:25.099 --> 00:12:26.594
So we had a long conversation.

00:12:26.594 --> 00:12:29.237
You know, I was much heavier then.

00:12:29.237 --> 00:12:31.472
And I hadn't put runnin'
shoes on in over a year.

00:12:31.472 --> 00:12:32.961
- How heavy are you at this point?

00:12:32.961 --> 00:12:35.282
- I'm around, between 240 to 270.

00:12:35.282 --> 00:12:36.519
- Whoa.
- I'm in there,

00:12:36.519 --> 00:12:37.509
I'm in that range.

00:12:37.509 --> 00:12:40.691
My weight has varied a
lot through the Seal teams

00:12:40.691 --> 00:12:43.506
and out of the Seal teams,
so I was a heavy guy.

00:12:43.506 --> 00:12:45.288
But the long and short of it all was,

00:12:45.288 --> 00:12:47.688
I hadn't put runnin'
shoes on in over a year.

00:12:47.688 --> 00:12:49.383
I was big time power lifter.

00:12:49.383 --> 00:12:51.321
I lifted weights heavy, that's what I did.

00:12:51.321 --> 00:12:52.434
- Right.
- I got back home

00:12:52.434 --> 00:12:54.916
from Iraq, went straight
to free fall school,

00:12:54.916 --> 00:12:56.690
and then this happened.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:58.842
So, I called Chris
Costen up on a Wednesday.

00:12:58.842 --> 00:13:00.658
He says, "Look man, the
only way you can qualify

00:13:00.658 --> 00:13:04.574
"for my race is to run
100 miles at one time,

00:13:04.574 --> 00:13:06.728
"in 24 hours or less."

00:13:06.728 --> 00:13:09.903
There happened to be a race that Saturday.

00:13:09.903 --> 00:13:12.098
So, four days later.

00:13:12.098 --> 00:13:15.331
And he said, "If you qualify,"
by runnin' 100 miles or less

00:13:15.331 --> 00:13:17.685
in 24 hours, "I will
consider you my race."

00:13:17.685 --> 00:13:20.242
I'm going to cut to the chase,

00:13:20.242 --> 00:13:21.859
I signed up for this race.

00:13:21.859 --> 00:13:23.555
It was called the San Diego One Day,

00:13:23.555 --> 00:13:26.325
where you run around a
mile track for 24 hours

00:13:26.325 --> 00:13:27.848
to see how many miles you can get.

00:13:27.848 --> 00:13:29.681
My goal was 100 miles.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:32.580
So, I got to mile 70,

00:13:33.879 --> 00:13:37.013
and I cleared 70 miles
in like, 12, 13 hours.

00:13:37.013 --> 00:13:38.089
Pretty quickly.

00:13:38.089 --> 00:13:39.615
But I was done.

00:13:39.615 --> 00:13:42.555
My feet were broken, I
was, stress fractures,

00:13:42.555 --> 00:13:45.197
shin splints, muscles were tearing.

00:13:45.197 --> 00:13:46.641
I was in bad shape.

00:13:46.641 --> 00:13:48.704
I was eatin' Ritz crackers
and drinkin' Myoplex.

00:13:48.704 --> 00:13:49.611
(chuckles)

00:13:49.611 --> 00:13:50.808
That's all I had.

00:13:50.808 --> 00:13:52.463
No water, didn't know what the
hell I was doin' out there,

00:13:52.463 --> 00:13:54.816
had on some tube socks.

00:13:54.816 --> 00:13:55.843
It was just ridiculous.

00:13:55.843 --> 00:13:57.961
It was a clown show.

00:13:57.961 --> 00:14:02.356
So, I sat down at mile 70, and
at this time I was married.

00:14:02.356 --> 00:14:05.402
And I look at my wife, and I was like,

00:14:05.402 --> 00:14:07.069
"I'm messed up bad."

00:14:08.535 --> 00:14:11.764
So, I literally start to turn white.

00:14:11.764 --> 00:14:13.045
And when a black guy turns white,

00:14:13.045 --> 00:14:14.325
you're pretty (silence) up.

00:14:14.325 --> 00:14:15.158
(audience laughs)

00:14:15.158 --> 00:14:18.994
So, here I am, I'm
(silence) up in this chair,

00:14:18.994 --> 00:14:21.348
I'm at mile 70, they got
30 (silence) miles to go.

00:14:21.348 --> 00:14:22.379
I'm jacked up.

00:14:22.379 --> 00:14:24.287
I got to go to the bathroom
and the bathroom's like,

00:14:24.287 --> 00:14:26.022
20 feet from me.

00:14:26.022 --> 00:14:27.386
It's a port-a-potty.

00:14:27.386 --> 00:14:29.078
I can't get out of the (silence) chair.

00:14:29.078 --> 00:14:30.607
So, I'm peein' blood

00:14:30.607 --> 00:14:32.216
down my leg,
- Whoa.

00:14:32.216 --> 00:14:34.162
- Poopin' up my (silence) back,

00:14:34.162 --> 00:14:36.245
and I got 30 miles to go.

00:14:37.299 --> 00:14:39.822
And I can't stand up
'cause my blood pressure's

00:14:39.822 --> 00:14:40.854
all messed up.

00:14:40.854 --> 00:14:42.134
I'd been in three Hell Weeks,

00:14:42.134 --> 00:14:46.427
Ranger School, overcome so
many obstacles in my life.

00:14:46.427 --> 00:14:50.594
This last 30 miles of this
race is when I realized

00:14:51.924 --> 00:14:55.091
a human being is not so human anymore.

00:14:55.983 --> 00:14:59.705
We have the ability to go in such a space,

00:14:59.705 --> 00:15:02.843
if you're willin' to
suffer, and I mean suffer.

00:15:02.843 --> 00:15:05.771
Your brain and your body
once connected together

00:15:05.771 --> 00:15:07.467
can do anything.

00:15:07.467 --> 00:15:12.126
And this 30 miles was
the life changing moment.

00:15:12.126 --> 00:15:13.611
I was out of it.

00:15:13.611 --> 00:15:16.127
I was in the worst pain in my entire life.

00:15:16.127 --> 00:15:19.127
I was, to me, on the brink of death.

00:15:21.365 --> 00:15:24.713
And I was able to chunk this 30 damn miles

00:15:24.713 --> 00:15:26.041
into small pieces.

00:15:26.041 --> 00:15:27.567
I was so driven.

00:15:27.567 --> 00:15:30.216
And I'm not going to say motivated,
'cause motivation's crap.

00:15:30.216 --> 00:15:32.570
Motivation comes and goes.

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:34.837
When you're driven,
whatever's in front of you

00:15:34.837 --> 00:15:36.854
will get destroyed.

00:15:36.854 --> 00:15:40.538
So, I sat in this chair,
and I was so driven

00:15:40.538 --> 00:15:42.724
to succeed in this race.

00:15:42.724 --> 00:15:44.749
And at this time, everybody
goes, "Were you thinkin'

00:15:44.749 --> 00:15:46.731
"about the guys that died?"

00:15:46.731 --> 00:15:49.457
I'm not going to lie to you, I wasn't.

00:15:49.457 --> 00:15:51.874
This became a personal thing.

00:15:53.172 --> 00:15:55.114
This became me against this race,

00:15:55.114 --> 00:15:56.600
me against the kids that
had called me Nigger,

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:58.004
me against me.

00:15:58.004 --> 00:16:02.171
It just became something that
I took so violently personal.

00:16:03.889 --> 00:16:06.122
And I broke this thing
down into small pieces.

00:16:06.122 --> 00:16:07.984
I said, "Okay, I got to get nutrition.

00:16:07.984 --> 00:16:10.539
"I got to be able to stand up
before I can get off this curb

00:16:10.539 --> 00:16:13.437
"and get off this chair,
and be able to go 30 miles."

00:16:13.437 --> 00:16:15.538
So I went through all these
small steps, and I was able

00:16:15.538 --> 00:16:16.980
to stand up.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:20.121
And then from standing up, I
was literally walking around

00:16:20.121 --> 00:16:22.640
with my wife, at the time,
and she goes, "You're not

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:23.545
"going to make the time."

00:16:23.545 --> 00:16:27.837
She goes, "You're walking
like 37 minute miles."

00:16:27.837 --> 00:16:31.176
I got to mile 81, and the
second she said that I'm not

00:16:31.176 --> 00:16:34.649
going to make the time,
I ran the last 19 miles,

00:16:34.649 --> 00:16:36.066
non stop.

00:16:36.066 --> 00:16:38.715
And I can show you right now,
when we get done with this,

00:16:38.715 --> 00:16:40.989
matter of fact, I'm going
to show you right now.

00:16:40.989 --> 00:16:43.054
This was years ago,

00:16:43.054 --> 00:16:46.314
and I had to put compression tape

00:16:46.314 --> 00:16:47.721
- Whoa!
- on my ankle.

00:16:47.721 --> 00:16:49.554
So this was years ago.

00:16:50.659 --> 00:16:54.076
I had literally the size of half dollars.

00:16:55.284 --> 00:16:59.908
I had to get compression tape,
and I taped up my ankles,

00:16:59.908 --> 00:17:01.764
and I taped up my feet.

00:17:01.764 --> 00:17:03.245
And that's how I got through that race.

00:17:03.245 --> 00:17:04.403
- Was it like a hematoma?

00:17:04.403 --> 00:17:06.193
I mean, what was happening?
- See, what happened was,

00:17:06.193 --> 00:17:10.689
my shins hurt so bad from
havin' the stress fractures,

00:17:10.689 --> 00:17:13.698
that the only way I could
continue on was I taped it

00:17:13.698 --> 00:17:15.762
so I wasn't doin' the flexor motion

00:17:15.762 --> 00:17:17.929
that activates your shins.

00:17:19.436 --> 00:17:23.941
So, I taped my ankles and my
shins up, and I got that from,

00:17:23.941 --> 00:17:26.210
because in my third Hell Week,
they weren't going to let me

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:27.043
go back through,

00:17:27.043 --> 00:17:28.251
you know, train anymore.
- Right.

00:17:28.251 --> 00:17:31.815
- So, I literally went
through all of BUD/S,

00:17:31.815 --> 00:17:33.219
my last Seal Training,
with stress fractures

00:17:33.219 --> 00:17:34.294
and shin splints.

00:17:34.294 --> 00:17:37.548
And how I did it was I would
tape my ankles all the way

00:17:37.548 --> 00:17:39.317
up to my calf every morning.

00:17:39.317 --> 00:17:42.827
So, for the first hour,
the pain was excruciating.

00:17:42.827 --> 00:17:45.688
But what would happen is,
my feet would go numb.

00:17:45.688 --> 00:17:46.521
- Whoa.
- And I did that

00:17:46.521 --> 00:17:49.736
every single day for six months.

00:17:49.736 --> 00:17:50.682
- Whoa!

00:17:50.682 --> 00:17:52.372
- And that's how I got
through my third Hell Week,

00:17:52.372 --> 00:17:55.884
'cause I was so broken from the
first two that the commander

00:17:55.884 --> 00:17:58.069
said, "Hey, the CO said
this is your last time

00:17:58.069 --> 00:17:58.902
"we're sendin' you through."

00:17:58.902 --> 00:18:01.536
So that's how I got the idea to do that.

00:18:01.536 --> 00:18:04.305
So, with the right, and
people may listen to this

00:18:04.305 --> 00:18:07.980
and say, "This guy is
sadistic, he's crazy."

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:11.608
No, if you know how I came
up, you'll realize I was

00:18:11.608 --> 00:18:13.025
just a scared kid

00:18:14.466 --> 00:18:17.516
that found drive and passion
to be somethin' much better

00:18:17.516 --> 00:18:19.207
than what he thought he was.

00:18:19.207 --> 00:18:20.073
That's all it is.

00:18:20.073 --> 00:18:24.240
Self talk and visualization
are the two keys to my success.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.657
I believed, for that last 19 miles,

00:18:29.637 --> 00:18:31.662
I was indestructible.

00:18:31.662 --> 00:18:35.376
'Cause I took myself in that
chair, crappin' up my back,

00:18:35.376 --> 00:18:38.765
peein' blood down my leg, shin
splints, stress fractures.

00:18:38.765 --> 00:18:41.910
I used all that for
motivation, versus negativity,

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:43.190
I used it for motivation.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:47.441
I said to myself, "Who
on this (silence) earth

00:18:47.441 --> 00:18:50.126
"would still be going right now?

00:18:50.126 --> 00:18:50.959
"You are.

00:18:52.068 --> 00:18:52.901
"You are.

00:18:53.964 --> 00:18:56.273
"You got to be the hardest
(silence) on the planet."

00:18:56.273 --> 00:18:57.222
Is it true?

00:18:57.222 --> 00:18:58.913
I don't give a (silence).

00:18:58.913 --> 00:19:00.454
At that time,

00:19:00.454 --> 00:19:01.730
- Right.
- it got me

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.785
to the finish line of that (silence) race.

00:19:04.785 --> 00:19:06.191
I believed it.

00:19:06.191 --> 00:19:07.680
I believe it today.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:11.344
I believed it enough
to where my body said,

00:19:11.344 --> 00:19:13.697
"He's not going to stop."

00:19:13.697 --> 00:19:17.167
And that's, I took all
the negative things,

00:19:17.167 --> 00:19:19.145
"I need to go to the
hospital," this and that,

00:19:19.145 --> 00:19:20.300
and I used it all.

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:22.691
"Who the hell could even
get out of that chair?

00:19:22.691 --> 00:19:24.055
"You did.

00:19:24.055 --> 00:19:25.626
"Who the hell would
even think about tapin'

00:19:25.626 --> 00:19:26.905
"stress fractures up?

00:19:26.905 --> 00:19:27.738
"You did."

00:19:28.591 --> 00:19:30.982
All those things I used for motivation.

00:19:30.982 --> 00:19:32.017
What's the point of livin'?

00:19:32.017 --> 00:19:34.619
You know, a lot of us go
through our day to day routine,

00:19:34.619 --> 00:19:36.679
kiss the wife, kiss the
husband, whatever you're doin',

00:19:36.679 --> 00:19:38.331
and that's our life.

00:19:38.331 --> 00:19:39.859
You know, I believe
everybody's put on this earth

00:19:39.859 --> 00:19:42.960
for a journey, and everybody's
journey is different.

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:45.485
But most people don't, you
know, take their journey.

00:19:45.485 --> 00:19:48.085
They try and take a easier path.

00:19:48.085 --> 00:19:49.078
So, that's what it's all about.

00:19:49.078 --> 00:19:50.815
You just got to accept your
journey, and a lot of times

00:19:50.815 --> 00:19:53.704
your journey sucks, but
that's what you got to do.

00:19:53.704 --> 00:19:55.024
It was always about givin' back.

00:19:55.024 --> 00:19:57.215
You know, God gave me a gift.

00:19:57.215 --> 00:20:00.309
You know, a gift to kind of
achieve what I need to achieve.

00:20:00.309 --> 00:20:02.042
You know, a strong mind.

00:20:02.042 --> 00:20:04.350
And if I can kind of relay that
message to these young kids,

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:05.757
young men and women.

00:20:05.757 --> 00:20:08.899
It's just havin' these
events around, it just helps

00:20:08.899 --> 00:20:10.725
them out, helps them grow.

00:20:10.725 --> 00:20:11.775
Kids need mentors nowadays.

00:20:11.775 --> 00:20:12.608
- [Man] Yes.

00:20:12.608 --> 00:20:14.533
- And to hear a positive
message from people

00:20:14.533 --> 00:20:17.691
who don't have much, you know,
that, "Hey, I can do it."

00:20:17.691 --> 00:20:18.884
- [Man] Yes.

00:20:18.884 --> 00:20:19.725
- That's what it's all about.

00:20:19.725 --> 00:20:21.582
You don't need much to become a lot.

00:20:21.582 --> 00:20:25.111
(breathing heavily)

00:20:25.111 --> 00:20:27.915
I started doin' this,
triathlons and endurance sports,

00:20:27.915 --> 00:20:31.541
ultra runs, just to test my
soul, to see what I'm about.

00:20:31.541 --> 00:20:35.708
To see in 150 mile race, at
mile 75, when I feel really bad,

00:20:37.025 --> 00:20:40.444
that's when you know, that's
the only time you can find out

00:20:40.444 --> 00:20:41.846
what the human body's all about.

00:20:41.846 --> 00:20:43.170
What you're made of.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:46.232
To find out limits to myself.

00:20:46.232 --> 00:20:48.586
Limits to the human soul.

00:20:48.586 --> 00:20:51.890
And everyday, I'm tryin'
to see if I have limits.

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:53.953
(breathing heavily)

00:20:53.953 --> 00:20:57.953
(mysterious instrumental music)

00:21:00.632 --> 00:21:03.894
I run anywhere from 125, 150 in a week.

00:21:03.894 --> 00:21:07.811
So, I'll try to put in
about 450 miles a month.

00:21:08.717 --> 00:21:11.440
Usually, everyday, I wake up
at three o'clock in the morning

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.163
and I run anywhere from 10
to 15 miles in the morning.

00:21:14.163 --> 00:21:17.550
And then, I live about 25 miles from work.

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:20.931
So, I get my bicycle, commute in to work,

00:21:20.931 --> 00:21:22.956
do a normal work day.

00:21:22.956 --> 00:21:26.463
At lunch time, around
noon, I'll run again.

00:21:26.463 --> 00:21:29.155
Anywhere from five to eight
miles, whatever it may be.

00:21:29.155 --> 00:21:33.242
Come back, work, and around
5:30, I ride back home

00:21:33.242 --> 00:21:35.188
25 miles on my bike.

00:21:35.188 --> 00:21:37.624
If I'm feelin' good, you
know, when I get more miles,

00:21:37.624 --> 00:21:40.514
I get off the bike and do
like a short three or four

00:21:40.514 --> 00:21:41.931
or five mile run.

00:21:43.321 --> 00:21:44.433
That's my everyday life.

00:21:44.433 --> 00:21:47.211
So, I don't just train like an hour or so.

00:21:47.211 --> 00:21:50.101
I train until somethin' is uncomfortable.

00:21:50.101 --> 00:21:52.832
And then, that's when
you know who you are.

00:21:52.832 --> 00:21:56.052
(footsteps landing)

00:21:56.052 --> 00:21:58.157
The only easy day was yesterday.

00:21:58.157 --> 00:21:59.804
It just never ends.

00:21:59.804 --> 00:22:01.954
I mean, I've done some races where you're

00:22:01.954 --> 00:22:06.905
on a one mile track, and you'll
run for 48 hours straight.

00:22:06.905 --> 00:22:10.330
So imagine runnin' 48 hours
and run a one mile track.

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:12.070
And it's not like you're
sleepin', you're running

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:15.220
for 48 hours to see how
many miles you can get.

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:17.720
Want to talk about mind torture?

00:22:19.652 --> 00:22:22.224
My life doesn't have a finish line.

00:22:22.224 --> 00:22:24.903
So, when I cross the finish
line of the Iron Man,

00:22:24.903 --> 00:22:27.646
doesn't matter 'cause I
know that two or three weeks

00:22:27.646 --> 00:22:29.010
from now, I'll be doin'
somethin' else to put

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:30.761
my body through more pain.

00:22:30.761 --> 00:22:33.677
(footsteps landing)

00:22:33.677 --> 00:22:35.297
I believe, you know,
everything I try to do

00:22:35.297 --> 00:22:37.789
is kind of defined by excellence.

00:22:37.789 --> 00:22:40.736
But, I don't think I ever
reach that excellence myself

00:22:40.736 --> 00:22:43.468
'cause I have my standards
of my life really high,

00:22:43.468 --> 00:22:44.752
so I don't think I ever reach it.

00:22:44.752 --> 00:22:46.705
But I try to every day.

00:22:46.705 --> 00:22:50.705
(mysterious instrumental music)

00:22:51.588 --> 00:22:53.787
You have to be a good individual first

00:22:53.787 --> 00:22:56.273
before you can be a good team player.

00:22:56.273 --> 00:22:58.228
I know for a fact how bad Hell Week is.

00:22:58.228 --> 00:23:00.459
I went through it a couple times.

00:23:00.459 --> 00:23:02.995
It's probably the most
brutal military training

00:23:02.995 --> 00:23:04.443
anybody can go through.

00:23:04.443 --> 00:23:06.602
So everybody to my left,
and everybody to my right

00:23:06.602 --> 00:23:07.937
they've been through this.

00:23:07.937 --> 00:23:10.300
So I know mentally, you
know, these individuals

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.667
are extremely hard.

00:23:12.667 --> 00:23:15.365
And when stuff gets bad, and you know,

00:23:15.365 --> 00:23:18.604
any situation, I know
these guys have my back.

00:23:18.604 --> 00:23:21.604
(footsteps landing)

00:23:24.638 --> 00:23:25.828
(breathing heavily)

00:23:25.828 --> 00:23:28.030
I've done a lot of things
I thought were impossible,

00:23:28.030 --> 00:23:30.697
and nothin's impossible anymore.

00:23:32.173 --> 00:23:33.290
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:37.443
I made this video because
MGOP Official asked me to.

00:23:37.443 --> 00:23:39.141
If there's someone you'd like to vote on

00:23:39.141 --> 00:23:41.416
for the next Top 10, check out
the link in the description,

00:23:41.416 --> 00:23:43.361
and go and have your say.

00:23:43.361 --> 00:23:44.973
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:23:44.973 --> 00:23:47.000
to Michael Shawn Phillips
from Lead the Team.

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:48.790
Michael, thank you so
much for picking up a copy

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:51.937
of my book, Your One Word,
and for doing the review

00:23:51.937 --> 00:23:52.892
on your website.

00:23:52.892 --> 00:23:54.719
I really appreciate the
support, and I'm so glad

00:23:54.719 --> 00:23:55.916
that you enjoyed the book.

00:23:55.916 --> 00:23:57.038
So, thank you guys, again, for watching.

00:23:57.038 --> 00:23:58.395
I believe in you.

00:23:58.395 --> 00:24:00.471
I hope you'll continue
to believe in yourself,

00:24:00.471 --> 00:24:02.713
and do whatever your One Word is.

00:24:02.713 --> 00:24:04.873
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:24:04.873 --> 00:24:06.956
(swoosh)

00:24:11.012 --> 00:24:14.047
- What power have you
found in the darkness?

00:24:14.047 --> 00:24:16.235
- First off, before I answer
that question, I want to say,

00:24:16.235 --> 00:24:18.318
everybody listen to this.

00:24:19.763 --> 00:24:23.261
I'm the happiest on the planet earth.

00:24:23.261 --> 00:24:25.951
So people may take this,
as so many people do.

00:24:25.951 --> 00:24:28.270
We live in a very weakened society.

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:30.799
So when they hear a
throw back guy like me,

00:24:30.799 --> 00:24:35.212
from back in the ancient
days of Garanimals,

00:24:35.212 --> 00:24:37.371
they often think, "This
guy is just whatever."

00:24:37.371 --> 00:24:41.224
So if you think that I'm some
unhappy guy, you're wrong.

00:24:41.224 --> 00:24:43.463
Havin' lived the life I've lived,

00:24:43.463 --> 00:24:47.358
and seeing the other side,
not being afraid to attack

00:24:47.358 --> 00:24:50.762
what was in front of
me, has made me happy.

00:24:50.762 --> 00:24:51.675
- Say that again.

00:24:51.675 --> 00:24:54.294
In fact, let me make sure I understood it.

00:24:54.294 --> 00:24:57.252
Getting to the point where
you're not afraid to face

00:24:57.252 --> 00:24:58.500
the thing on the other side of the door

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:00.986
that wants to attack
you, has made you happy.

00:25:00.986 --> 00:25:01.900
- Right.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.241
Right.
- It's really powerful.

00:25:03.241 --> 00:25:04.856
I hope people heard that.
- Right.

00:25:04.856 --> 00:25:05.973
That made me very happy.

00:25:05.973 --> 00:25:09.289
So, basically, I just don't
walk around with a dad-gum smile

00:25:09.289 --> 00:25:10.569
on my face all the damn time.

00:25:10.569 --> 00:25:11.526
So, you know, Merry Christmas.

00:25:11.526 --> 00:25:12.767
(laughing)

00:25:12.767 --> 00:25:16.385
But basically, what the dark side is,

00:25:16.385 --> 00:25:17.635
is, we all have

00:25:19.618 --> 00:25:20.701
a cookie jar.

00:25:21.772 --> 00:25:24.130
And we all have a jar of (silence).

00:25:24.130 --> 00:25:25.418
(laughing)

00:25:25.418 --> 00:25:27.204
That's it's official name.

00:25:27.204 --> 00:25:28.866
It's a jar of (silence)
man, where (silence) just,

00:25:28.866 --> 00:25:31.033
it just ain't goin' right.

00:25:31.988 --> 00:25:34.843
And in Hell Week, what
they do in Hell Week,

00:25:34.843 --> 00:25:38.063
'cause this is where I
really went to the dark side.

00:25:38.063 --> 00:25:41.457
What they do in Hell Week
is they design Hell Week

00:25:41.457 --> 00:25:43.040
to find your flaws.

00:25:44.482 --> 00:25:46.146
And they do a really good job of that.

00:25:46.146 --> 00:25:48.093
It's 130 hours of continuous training.

00:25:48.093 --> 00:25:49.953
You may get two hours of sleep.

00:25:49.953 --> 00:25:52.489
And they beat the (silence)
out of you and find everything

00:25:52.489 --> 00:25:55.481
wrong with your mentality.

00:25:55.481 --> 00:25:58.431
And then, they start Hell Week.

00:25:58.431 --> 00:26:00.132
And that's the beauty of it.

00:26:00.132 --> 00:26:04.612
And for me, I'm not some
nasty guy, giving guy.

00:26:04.612 --> 00:26:08.176
You know, I don't have a great
bit of talent in anything.

00:26:08.176 --> 00:26:11.992
So what got me through horrible
times was the dark side.

00:26:11.992 --> 00:26:15.886
Was, I created, my name is David Goggins.

00:26:15.886 --> 00:26:17.386
I created Goggins.

00:26:18.465 --> 00:26:22.817
Goggins is the guy that
can take anything you put

00:26:22.817 --> 00:26:24.146
in front of him.

00:26:24.146 --> 00:26:27.473
You want to break my
(silence) legs, so be it.

00:26:27.473 --> 00:26:31.291
I have a way of goin' to a
place, like I did in that race,

00:26:31.291 --> 00:26:33.902
where all the pain and
sufferin' that they put on top

00:26:33.902 --> 00:26:37.385
of me in Hell Week, I will
reverse that pain and suffering

00:26:37.385 --> 00:26:39.552
and I will take your soul.

00:26:40.484 --> 00:26:43.222
So, every instructor that
put me through BUD/S,

00:26:43.222 --> 00:26:47.604
my job, what drove me, was
I wanted you to go home

00:26:47.604 --> 00:26:51.456
that night, after you beat the
living (silence) out of me,

00:26:51.456 --> 00:26:53.655
and I smiled in your face.

00:26:53.655 --> 00:26:56.690
I wanted you to feel worse
than I did, and you were goin'

00:26:56.690 --> 00:27:00.344
home to a nice, warm bed,
with your wife, or your kids,

00:27:00.344 --> 00:27:04.378
and a nice meal, and I was
still out there in the grip.

00:27:04.378 --> 00:27:06.533
Sufferin' for another hundred hours.

00:27:06.533 --> 00:27:10.175
I wanted you to think about me,
knowing that I'm comfortable

00:27:10.175 --> 00:27:13.039
bein' very un (silence) comfortable.

00:27:13.039 --> 00:27:14.988
And I want you to think
about when you went through

00:27:14.988 --> 00:27:16.112
(silence) Hell Week.

00:27:16.112 --> 00:27:19.227
How uncomfortable you were and
how bad you wanted to quit.

00:27:19.227 --> 00:27:21.966
Knowing I'm not thinkin'
that (silence) way.

00:27:21.966 --> 00:27:25.971
So the dark side is
somethin' that I've designed.

00:27:25.971 --> 00:27:30.160
It's a evil place I can
go that very few things

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:31.160
can hurt me.

00:27:32.603 --> 00:27:35.055
I use the hurt you're tryin' to put on me,

00:27:35.055 --> 00:27:37.996
I flip it upside down, and use it.

00:27:37.996 --> 00:27:40.142
You're tryin' to use it for Kryptonite?

00:27:40.142 --> 00:27:41.605
No.

00:27:41.605 --> 00:27:43.270
It's power pillows for me.

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:44.763
I'm usin' it for strength.

00:27:44.763 --> 00:27:47.862
I flip negative into
positive, that's all it is.

